 *OOP Concept Demonstrated* 

The program demonstrates runtime polymorphism using the virtual ""displayInfo()"" function. The base-class "Vehicle" pointer can refer to different derived vehicle objects, and the appropriate overridden function is called at runtime.

 *Objective* 

To understand how inheritance, runtime polymorphism, encapsulation, virtual functions, and smart pointers can be used to design a flexible vehicle fleet management system.

 *Key Concepts Demonstrated* 

- Inheritance – "Truck", "DeliveryVan", and "Bike" inherit common features from "Vehicle".
- Polymorphism – Each vehicle type provides its own implementation of "displayInfo()".
- Function Overriding – Derived classes override the virtual "displayInfo()" function.
- Encapsulation – Vehicle-specific data is maintained within the respective classes.
- Virtual Function – Enables runtime selection of the appropriate "displayInfo()" implementation.
- Smart Pointers – "unique_ptr" provides automatic memory management.
- Vector – Stores multiple vehicle objects in a single fleet collection.
- Constructors – Used to initialize vehicle and type-specific information.
- Virtual Destructor – Ensures proper destruction of derived objects.
